  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Standards · North America · Last reviewed 2026-09-22</p>
      <h1>SAE J537: The North American Cranking Amps Standard</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        SAE J537 is the document behind nearly every CCA number printed on a North American battery. It defines
        <strong>how</strong> cranking amps are measured — which is why a "600 CCA" label means something precise:
        <strong>600 amps for 30 seconds at −18°C (0°F), staying at or above 7.2 volts</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> SAE J537 (Society of Automotive Engineers) is the North American standard for
        12V lead-acid starter-battery <strong>cranking performance and capacity</strong>. Its three cranking ratings are
        the same 30-second, ≥7.2V test at three temperatures: <strong>CCA at −18°C (0°F)</strong>, <strong>CA at 0°C (32°F)</strong>
        and <strong>HCA at 27°C (80°F)</strong>. When a US or Canadian label says "CCA" without qualification, it is a J537 number.
      </div>

      <h2>What SAE J537 actually tests</h2>
      <p>
        The standard's core is the <strong>cold cranking test</strong>: a fully-charged battery is cooled to −18°C, then
        discharged at the rated current for 30 seconds. It passes if the terminal voltage stays at or above
        <strong>7.2V</strong> — 1.2 volts per cell, the floor below which a starter can no longer reliably turn an engine.
        The rating is the <em>largest current the battery can hold at that floor for that time</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        J537 also defines the <strong>20-hour capacity test</strong> (the "Ah" number, discharged over 20 hours to a
        specified end voltage) and the <strong>reserve capacity test</strong> — minutes at 25 amps until the battery
        drops below 10.5V. CCA, Ah and reserve capacity are three different measurements of the same battery; see
        <a href="/lead-acid/cca-vs-ah/">CCA vs Ah</a> and <a href="/lead-acid/reserve-capacity/">reserve capacity explained</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The temperature ladder: CCA, CA, HCA</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Rating</th><th>Temperature</th><th>Test</th><th>Relative number</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>CCA</strong> (cold cranking amps)</td><td>−18°C (0°F)</td><td>30 s, ≥7.2V</td><td>Baseline</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>CA</strong> (cranking amps)</td><td>0°C (32°F)</td><td>30 s, ≥7.2V</td><td><strong>Higher</strong> (~20–25% above CCA)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>HCA</strong> (hot cranking amps)</td><td>27°C (80°F)</td><td>30 s, ≥7.2V</td><td><strong>Highest</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        Same battery, same 30-second floor, three temperatures — the warmer the test, the bigger the printed number.
        See <a href="/lead-acid/cca/cca-vs-ca/">CCA vs CA vs MCA</a> for the full temperature ladder.
      </p>

      <h2>SAE J537 vs EN 50342 vs JIS D5301</h2>
      <p>
        "CCA" is a number under a method, and the methods differ. SAE J537 and
        <a href="/standards/en-50342/">EN 50342</a> both test at −18°C, but EN uses a 10-second discharge with a 7.5V
        cut-off, while SAE holds 30 seconds to 7.2V — small differences that shift the printed number.
        <a href="/standards/jis-d5301/">JIS D5301</a> tests at −15°C, which is why JIS figures read higher for the same
        physical battery. The practical rule: <strong>compare SAE to SAE, EN to EN, JIS to JIS</strong>, and never
        cross-convert with a fixed multiplier (see <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">what CCA is</a> for the full comparison).
      </p>

      <h2>How the CCA test is run</h2>
      <p>
        The test sequence is: fully charge the battery → cool it to −18°C in a temperature-controlled chamber (usually
        with an air bath) → record the open-circuit voltage → discharge at the rated current for 30 seconds while
        logging terminal voltage every second → pass if the voltage never falls below 7.2V. The same procedure runs at
        0°C for CA and 27°C for HCA. The full step-by-step is on the testing hub's
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/cca-testing/">how CCA is tested</a> page.
      </p>

      <h2>What J537 governs beyond cranking</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Capacity (C20)</strong> — ampere-hours over a 20-hour discharge, the "Ah" figure on the label.</li>
        <li><strong>Reserve capacity</strong> — minutes of 25A discharge before the battery reaches 10.5V.</li>
        <li><strong>Charge acceptance</strong> — how readily the battery takes charge, which matters for start-stop duty.</li>
        <li><strong>Life/endurance requirements</strong> — cycle and vibration expectations for starter service.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        BCI group sizing (Group 24, 31, 65…) lives beside J537: the group number fixes the physical envelope and
        terminal layout, and J537 fixes how the ratings inside that envelope are measured. Both are needed to read a
        North American label completely — see <a href="/lead-acid/battery-group-sizes/">battery group sizes explained</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>SAE J537 is the <strong>North American</strong> starter-battery standard: CCA at −18°C, 30 s, ≥7.2V.</li>
          <li>CA (0°C) and HCA (27°C) are the same test warmer — <strong>the warmer the test, the higher the number</strong>.</li>
          <li>J537 also defines <strong>capacity (Ah), reserve capacity and charge acceptance</strong> — not cranking alone.</li>
          <li>An unqualified "CCA" in North America is a J537 figure; <strong>never compare it to a JIS or EN figure</strong> without re-testing.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>What procurement buyers should check</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Does the datasheet state the rating standard — SAE J537 — or just print "CCA" with no basis?</li>
        <li>Is the claimed CCA verified by an independent lab test, not only the manufacturer's self-declaration? See <a href="/procurement/verify-cca-claims-bulk-purchase/">verifying CCA claims in bulk purchase</a>.</li>
        <li>When comparing an Asian supplier's JIS-rated battery against a North American SAE-rated spec, is the test temperature difference (−15°C vs −18°C) being handled?</li>
        <li>Is the quoted capacity tied to the C20 rate, and reserve capacity listed for fleet vehicles with engine-off loads?</li>
        <li>Does the supplier's <a href="/procurement/how-to-read-battery-datasheet/">datasheet</a> give the test standard per rating, or one merged number?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What does SAE J537 measure?</h3>
      <p>Cranking performance and capacity of 12V automotive starter batteries. Its CCA test holds the battery at −18°C (0°F) and requires it to deliver a stated current for 30 seconds without dropping below 7.2V (1.2V per cell). It also defines CA (cranking amps at 0°C) and HCA (hot cranking amps at 27°C).</p>
      <h3>How does SAE J537 CCA compare to JIS D5301 CCA?</h3>
      <p>SAE tests colder. JIS D5301 measures CCA at −15°C, SAE J537 at −18°C, so the same battery prints a higher CCA under JIS than under SAE. There is no universal conversion factor — compare figures only within one standard.</p>
      <h3>What is the difference between CCA, CA and HCA in SAE J537?</h3>
      <p>Temperature. CCA is at −18°C (0°F), CA is at 0°C (32°F) and HCA is at 27°C (80°F), all for 30 seconds to the same 7.2V floor. Warmer tests print higher numbers, so CA is typically 20–25% above CCA for the same battery.</p>
      <h3>Is SAE J537 the same as BCI group sizing?</h3>
      <p>No. BCI group numbers (Group 24, 31, 65…) define the physical case envelope and terminal layout; SAE J537 defines how the electrical ratings are tested. A complete North American battery specification needs both: the group for fit, the standard for ratings.</p>

